  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You are given a set SSS of NNN distinct integer points in the 2D2D2D plane. Initially, each point has a color, either `red` or `green`.\n\nYou have to perform some number of operations (possibly 000) on this set of points such that all points are `green` after all operations.\n\nAn operation is:\n\n\nChoose some line that does not pass through any of the points in SSS. This line should also not pass through the origin.\nThe line divides the space into two half-planes. Let QQQ be the set of all points in SSS that are also in the same half-plane as the origin.\nFlip the colors of all the points in QQQ. That is, if a point in QQQ had the color `red` then it becomes `green` and vice versa.\nWhat is the minimum number of operations that need to be done to convert all the points to `green` color?\n\nIt can be proven that it's always possible to convert any set of colored points to the green color in a finite number of operations.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of each test case contains a single integer NNN - the size of SSS.\nThe next line contains a string of length NNN where the iii-th character of the string is R\\texttt{R}R if the iii-th point is `red` and G\\texttt{G}G if it's `green`.\nThe iii-th line of next NNN lines contains two integers xi,yix_i, y_ixi​,yi​ - the coordinates of the iii-th point of set SSS.\n\n\nOutput Format\n\nFor every test case, you must print the minimum number of operations needed to convert all points to green color.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤N≤171 \\le N \\le 171≤N≤17",
      "−106≤xi,yi≤106-10^6 \\leq x_i,y_i \\leq 10^6−106≤xi​,yi​≤106",
      "(xi,yi)≠(0,0)(x_i, y_i) \\neq (0,0)(xi​,yi​)=(0,0) for all iii",
      "All points are pairwise distinct"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "9\nRRGRGGRGR\n2 -4\n-9 5\n-2 -1\n-6 2\n7 -2\n-9 -6\n3 -5\n5 0\n0 -3",
        "output": "2",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nThe below image explains the operations needed. The black line is the line selected in the operation and the blue point is the origin."
      }
    ]
  },
